WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.229 --> 00:00:07.889
Continue working on your project or open the file named to-materials if you need to catch up.

2
00:00:08.740 --> 00:00:12.539
Essentially, this file starts with the one from the last video left off.

3
00:00:13.140 --> 00:00:22.379
Here, we'll use ready-made PBR materials from the Materials library to replace incoming materials from the DCC app, in this case Revit.

4
00:00:23.519 --> 00:00:30.820
The process is simple. You can first sample a material using the Material Picker, such as the one on the curved walls.

5
00:00:31.929 --> 00:00:36.429
Once the material is displayed on the right, you can make adjustments to it.

6
00:00:37.250 --> 00:00:41.789
Better yet, you can replace it altogether with a better one from the Material library.

7
00:00:42.450 --> 00:00:49.810
For that, you can go to the Materials subsection in the Asset library and choose a category such as Concrete.

8
00:00:51.280 --> 00:00:55.280
You can hover over thumbnails to get a better representation of materials.

9
00:00:56.229 --> 00:01:06.170
When you decide on a material, you can drag and drop it on an object in the viewport, or on the existing material in the right panel.

10
00:01:07.310 --> 00:01:09.810
This replaces the old material with the new.

11
00:01:10.760 --> 00:01:19.260
Right off the bat, you'll notice a UV problem. This happens sometimes with DCC apps that use a different unit system than Twinmotion.

12
00:01:20.469 --> 00:01:24.969
Here, we'll need to adjust the UV Scale or Tiling to make this work.

13
00:01:26.719 --> 00:01:31.019
Adjust the scale slider and note that it caps at 10.

14
00:01:32.120 --> 00:01:38.219
If you need to go beyond that number, you can simply type in a value, in this case 20.

15
00:01:42.819 --> 00:01:51.719
You can edit other values as well. For example, you can make the color a bit lighter by increasing the luminosity to about 80%.

16
00:01:56.290 --> 00:02:03.969
Let's try another one. Let's apply the Polished Concrete 2 material to the cafe floor.

17
00:02:04.700 --> 00:02:07.799
And notice how it also applies to the main building floor.

18
00:02:08.810 --> 00:02:15.710
These two objects initially share the same incoming material from Revit, and therefore are both affected by the new material.

19
00:02:16.469 --> 00:02:23.969
We still need to bring about a few edits, but first let's also apply that same material to the walls of the bar within the theater area.

20
00:02:25.590 --> 00:02:28.590
You can do this from the displayed material on the right panel.

21
00:02:32.319 --> 00:02:37.689
Now you can adjust the scale. I'll set mine at around 10.

22
00:02:45.139 --> 00:02:53.490
Let's keep going. This time, we'll step back and go to the Ground category.

23
00:02:55.590 --> 00:03:04.090
Under Manmade, use an Asphalt material, such as Asphalt 4, to apply onto the roof of the cafe.

24
00:03:04.819 --> 00:03:09.620
Again, it replaces all surfaces sharing the original incoming material from Revit.

25
00:03:13.169 --> 00:03:18.770
And again, it requires adjusting the UV Scale a bit. A value of 10 should do.

26
00:03:20.129 --> 00:03:25.530
The Asphalt is a bit too clean. Sometimes it pays off to dirty it up a little bit.

27
00:03:26.509 --> 00:03:33.270
All you need is to increase the amount of grunge. I'll set mine to about 35 or 40%.

28
00:03:33.770 --> 00:03:37.069
This technique also breaks the monotony of UV tiling.

29
00:03:41.090 --> 00:03:43.490
Let's do a few more around the theater area.

30
00:03:46.199 --> 00:03:50.599
Perhaps apply a Wave Cobblestone material to the walking pathways.

31
00:03:54.520 --> 00:03:57.319
And adjusting scale and grunge like earlier.

32
00:04:03.009 --> 00:04:15.870
You can also add a Soft Concrete material for the stands, and possibly the front steps.

33
00:04:20.370 --> 00:04:25.970
If you've already used the material and adjusted it, and then decide to use it again on a different object,

34
00:04:26.269 --> 00:04:30.170
such as the wall material to replicate onto the stage for example,

35
00:04:31.060 --> 00:04:37.060
then you can simply sample it first, and then drag it from the Properties panel onto the stage.

36
00:04:38.620 --> 00:04:43.819
In this case, all adjustments to UVs and color and the likes are also retained.

37
00:04:45.370 --> 00:04:48.370
Take a moment to experiment and add materials to the scene.

38
00:04:49.709 --> 00:04:57.639
Try a glass material on the curtain wall, or a metal material on the mullions.

39
00:05:01.660 --> 00:05:05.060
Keep in mind you can tint any material that you use in the scene.

40
00:05:15.139 --> 00:05:22.240
Consider applying the same metal to other objects such as ramps, handrails, and planters maybe.

41
00:05:27.600 --> 00:05:45.360
Try a wood material on the sides of the planters, or some grass where appropriate.

42
00:05:45.759 --> 00:06:00.199
Experiment on your own in other areas of the project as well.

43
00:06:30.139 --> 00:06:36.839
So far, material substitution has been going well, but there are situations that can catch you off guard.

44
00:06:39.740 --> 00:06:42.240
Consider this area on the cafe floor.

45
00:06:43.089 --> 00:06:46.089
It has the same Asphalt material as the roof.

46
00:06:47.170 --> 00:06:54.670
If you try to change that by assigning another material to that patch only, say a Cobblestone material of some sort,

47
00:06:56.930 --> 00:07:00.129
it will replace the Asphalt material anywhere else in the scene.

48
00:07:00.699 --> 00:07:03.199
That's the nature of the Material Substitution tool.

49
00:07:04.399 --> 00:07:06.699
Press Ctrl-Z to undo the last step.

50
00:07:07.560 --> 00:07:13.660
You can prevent that from happening by switching the Replace Material Mode to Apply to Object.

51
00:07:17.259 --> 00:07:21.959
Try it again and see that the cobblestones are only applied to that patch of ground.

52
00:07:24.319 --> 00:07:27.620
Similar problems may appear when dealing with instances.

53
00:07:27.920 --> 00:07:30.519
Look at the curtain wall panels on the main building.

54
00:07:33.170 --> 00:07:41.370
Even though it just set the Material Mode to Apply to Object, note what happens when you apply a material to one of these panels on the main façade.

55
00:07:42.220 --> 00:07:46.019
You'll notice that the materials apply to many of them simultaneously.

56
00:07:46.720 --> 00:07:52.920
That's because even though these panels are separate entities, they are all based on the same Revit family.

57
00:07:53.699 --> 00:07:59.500
This means they get imported as instances and therefore are treated the same way within Twinmotion.

58
00:08:00.420 --> 00:08:04.920
To apply different materials to them, you need first to break the instancing.

59
00:08:05.420 --> 00:08:10.220
Select all the non-glass panels on the main façade with the help of the Ctrl key.

60
00:08:10.889 --> 00:08:15.689
Right-click on one of them in the scene graph and choose Break Instance.

61
00:08:16.990 --> 00:08:21.089
From this point on, you can apply different materials to the different panels.

62
00:08:30.170 --> 00:08:31.970
One final note to point out.

63
00:08:32.600 --> 00:08:38.299
Beware of your UV coordinates between Twinmotion, OpenStreetMaps and your DCC app.

64
00:08:39.600 --> 00:08:46.600
You've already noticed that in most cases, scaling factors would necessarily apply to work well with Revit geometry.

65
00:08:47.490 --> 00:08:53.090
But if you were to sample one of those materials, such as the lawn material you used earlier for example,

66
00:08:54.039 --> 00:09:02.470
and then try to apply it on a non-Revit object such as the green areas of the urban surroundings, the same settings would not work.

67
00:09:03.419 --> 00:09:06.019
That's a discrepancy with the UV coordinates.

68
00:09:06.519 --> 00:09:14.820
When that happens, you can try to reapply UV coordinates from the main toolbar, overwriting the object's UVs.

69
00:09:15.929 --> 00:09:20.330
You'd need to reapply the material and then adjust its parameters globally.

70
00:09:23.769 --> 00:09:25.470
This is starting to shape up.

71
00:09:26.000 --> 00:09:31.700
In the next video, you'll experiment with other asset types that the Asset Library has to offer.
